1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,119
Time became a typed contract. That helps
when values are valid. It does not help
when the world refuses to cooperate.
Suppose your service must load a
configuration file at startup.

2
00:00:10,239 --> 00:00:19,359
The happy path reads, parses, and
continues. The unhappy path is just as
real: the file is missing, the disk is
full, permissions deny access.

3
00:00:19,479 --> 00:00:29,879
Ignore failure and you get a crash with no
context — or a silent wrong default that
looks healthy until noon traffic arrives.
So how should a method announce that it
could not do its job?

4
00:00:30,059 --> 00:00:43,779
Exceptions are control flow for failure.
Treat them as part of the API, not an
embarrassing side channel mentioned only
in comments. Java splits checked and
unchecked exceptions, and the split is a
design choice.

5
00:00:43,899 --> 00:00:50,459
Checked exceptions must be declared or
handled — they push the caller to
acknowledge a failure mode the type system
can see.

6
00:00:50,579 --> 00:01:04,139
Unchecked exceptions — subclasses of
RuntimeException — need not be declared;
they often represent programming mistakes
or failures you do not expect every caller
to recover from locally. Neither family is
always better.

7
00:01:04,259 --> 00:01:14,219
The question is whether the failure is
part of the method's honest contract. Here
is wrapping with context instead of
leaking a low-level type through every
layer: Look at this java for a moment.

8
00:01:14,339 --> 00:01:26,699
read() can fail with IOException. Catching
and throwing UncheckedIOException with a
message and e as the cause preserves the
original failure while changing how
callers must react. The cause chain
matters.

9
00:01:26,819 --> 00:01:37,459
Catch and throw a new exception without
the cause, and you erase the forensic
trail production debugging needs. Do not
swallow exceptions: Look at this java for
a moment.

10
00:01:37,579 --> 00:01:46,060
An empty catch makes the program continue
as if success happened. If you catch,
either recover meaningfully, translate
with context, or rethrow.

11
00:01:46,179 --> 00:01:57,299
Logging and continuing can be valid — only
as intentional policy, not as a reflex to
silence the compiler. finally exists for
cleanup that must run whether the try
succeeded or threw.

12
00:01:57,419 --> 00:02:06,339
You will still see it in older code: Look
at this java for a moment. This pattern is
easy to almost get right and hard to get
perfect when close() itself throws.

13
00:02:06,459 --> 00:02:16,779
Modern Java prefers try-with-resources
when the thing you opened implements
AutoCloseable — and that story deserves
its own space, because the close path has
subtle failure modes.

14
00:02:16,899 --> 00:02:26,819
Hold the rule lightly: if the point of the
block is "close this resource," a better
tool is coming. Meaningful exception types
are the other half of API design.

15
00:02:26,939 --> 00:02:31,219
A generic RuntimeException("failed") tells
the caller almost nothing.

16
00:02:31,339 --> 00:02:45,819
A domain exception —
OrderNotFoundException,
InvalidTaxRateException — lets upper
layers choose policy: retry, skip, surface
to the user, page an operator. Create
types when the failure mode is part of
your vocabulary.

17
00:02:45,939 --> 00:02:55,939
What if we use exceptions for ordinary
control flow — throwing to mean "not
found" on a hot path that is usually
empty? It can work mechanically and still
be the wrong shape.

18
00:02:56,059 --> 00:03:08,379
Exceptions are expensive compared with a
normal branch, and they obscure the
expected path. Prefer return values,
Optional, or explicit result types for
common, non-exceptional outcomes.

19
00:03:08,499 --> 00:03:20,819
Reserve throws for genuine failure. Think
about the call stack. A low-level I/O
helper throws IOException. A repository
catches it and throws ConfigLoadException
with the path and the cause.

20
00:03:20,939 --> 00:03:27,539
A startup component decides whether to
abort or fall back. Each layer adds the
decision it is qualified to make.

21
00:03:27,659 --> 00:03:36,739
That is what "design exceptions like an
API" means — not inventing twenty
subclasses for fun, but placing failure
information where policy can use it.

22
00:03:36,859 --> 00:03:47,099
If every method only logs and returns null
on failure, you have not removed
exceptions. You have replaced them with a
quieter protocol callers must remember to
check.

23
00:03:47,219 --> 00:03:57,339
Sometimes that quieter protocol is
Optional — right for expected absence. For
true operational failure, an exception
that preserves context is often the
clearer contract.

24
00:03:57,459 --> 00:04:06,779
Once you accept that resources must open
and may fail, a sharper problem appears:
even on the success path, an open file,
socket, or stream must be closed.

25
00:04:06,899 --> 00:04:12,499
Doing that correctly in every finally is
tedious — and easy to get slightly wrong
when close itself throws.
